var tipuesearch = {"pages":[{"title":" TensorOmics ","text":"TensorOmics Tensor Omics is a high-performance framework for explainable, geometry-based analysis of multimodal omics and related high-dimensional datasets. Instead of relying on black-box models, it treats expression profiles, clinical measures, or socioeconomic indicators as vectors in semantically meaningful spaces (e.g. tissues, disease stages, conditions). By measuring distances, angles, projections, and trajectories in these spaces, Tensor Omics enables direct comparison of activity across genes, paralogs, sexes, species, or patient groups. This geometric approach makes complex multivariate patterns interpretable and reproducible while remaining robust to sparsity and noise. Designed for distributed high-performance computing, Tensor Omics is implemented in Fortran and C with OpenMP parallelisation, SIMD optimisation, and Fortran Coarrays, making the algorithms embarrassingly parallel and suitable for federated datasets where privacy and efficiency are critical. Scientific use cases include: detecting disease biomarkers and subtype-specific trajectories in medical data; quantifying divergence and neofunctionalization of gene duplicates in plant and animal transcriptomes; and reconstructing global gender-equality trajectories from socioeconomic indicators. Across these domains, Tensor Omics provides a unified, geometry-driven methodology for discovering explanatory patterns in heterogeneous, high-dimensional data. Key Features Geometry-based analysis : distances, angles, projections, and trajectory shifts are used as primary primitives. Explainable outputs : results are interpretable in terms of vector geometry rather than opaque model coefficients. Multi-modal integration : unifies transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics, clinical, or socioeconomic data within one framework. Parallel and federated : implemented in Fortran/C with OpenMP, SIMD, and coarrays for efficient large-scale computation on distributed datasets. Robust to sparsity and noise : percentile-based empirical thresholds and local geometric measures enable stability. Broad applications : demonstrated on medical biomarker discovery, gene duplication outcomes, developmental trajectories, and socioeconomic indicators. Tensor Omics shows that geometry, when treated not as preprocessing but as the central instrument of analysis, can open entirely new ways to read complex biological and social data — simple, transparent, and surprisingly powerful.","tags":"home","url":"index.html"},{"title":"hashmap_type – TensorOmics ","text":"type, public, extends(../../hashmap_base) :: hashmap_type Hashmap of character keys to integer(int32) values, using separate chaining over a\npower-of-two-sized bucket array. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int32), public :: size = 0 Number of buckets (always a power of two, see next_power_of_two ). integer(kind=int32), public :: count = 0 Number of key-value pairs currently stored. type(hashmap_node_type), public, pointer :: buckets (:) => null() Bucket array; buckets(i)%next heads the chain for bucket i (the bucket\nelements themselves only ever use their next pointer, never key / value ).","tags":"","url":"type/hashmap_type.html"},{"title":"hashset_type – TensorOmics ","text":"type, public, extends(../../hashmap_base) :: hashset_type Hashset of character keys, using separate chaining over a power-of-two-sized bucket array. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int32), public :: size = 0 Number of buckets (always a power of two, see next_power_of_two ). integer(kind=int32), public :: count = 0 Number of key-value pairs currently stored. type(hashset_node_type), public, pointer :: buckets (:) => null() Bucket array; buckets(i)%next heads the chain for bucket i (the bucket\nelements themselves only ever use their next pointer, never key / value ).","tags":"","url":"type/hashset_type.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_plain – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_plain(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, fitted_values, ierr) Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using the specified smoothing parameter and outputs the smoothed\nresponse array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n Total number of data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: y Response variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n, 1) :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_plain.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_plain_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_plain_expert(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, tmp_int_workspace, int_workspace_size, tmp_real_workspace, real_workspace_size, tmp_hat_diag, fitted_values, ierr) Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using the specified smoothing parameter and outputs the smoothed\nresponse array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n Total number of data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: y Response variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n, 1) :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(int_workspace_size) :: tmp_int_workspace Integer workspace array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: int_workspace_size Required size of the integer workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the int_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 10000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(real_workspace_size) :: tmp_real_workspace Real workspace array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: real_workspace_size Required size of the real workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the real_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 100000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_hat_diag Diagonal elements of the hat matrix real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_plain_expert.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_robust – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_robust(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, n_iters, fitted_values, ierr) Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using robust iterations to handle outliers.\nThe robust fitting process iterates n_iters times, each iteration:\n- Combines original weights with robust weights (down-weights from previous iteration)\n- Runs LOESS fitting with combined weights\n- Computes residuals (y - fitted values)\n- Updates robust weights using bisquare function (suppresses large residuals) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n Total number of data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: y Response variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n, 1) :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional :: n_iters Number of robust iterations\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe default value is 3_int32 . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_robust.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_robust_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_robust_expert(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, n_iters, tmp_int_workspace, int_workspace_size, tmp_real_workspace, real_workspace_size, tmp_hat_diag, tmp_robust_weights, tmp_combined_weights, tmp_residuals, tmp_permutation_indices, fitted_values, ierr) Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using robust iterations to handle outliers.\nThe robust fitting process iterates n_iters times, each iteration:\n- Combines original weights with robust weights (down-weights from previous iteration)\n- Runs LOESS fitting with combined weights\n- Computes residuals (y - fitted values)\n- Updates robust weights using bisquare function (suppresses large residuals) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n Total number of data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: y Response variable array real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n) :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n, 1) :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional :: n_iters Number of robust iterations\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe default value is 3_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(int_workspace_size) :: tmp_int_workspace Integer workspace array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: int_workspace_size Required size of the integer workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the int_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 10000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(real_workspace_size) :: tmp_real_workspace Real workspace array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: real_workspace_size Required size of the real workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the real_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 100000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_hat_diag Diagonal elements of the hat matrix real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_robust_weights Robust bisquare weights (updated each iteration, initialized to 1.0) real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_combined_weights Combined weights: product of user weights and robust weights (weights(i) * robust_weights(i)) real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_residuals Residuals (y - fitted_values), used to compute bisquare robust weights integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n) :: tmp_permutation_indices Permutation indices array (from NetLib bisquare weight computation) real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n) :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_robust_expert.html"},{"title":"euclidean_distance – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine euclidean_distance(vec1, vec2, n_elements, result, ierr) Calculates the L2 norm: result = sqrt(sum((vec1_i - vec2_i)**2)) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_elements) :: vec1 First expression vector real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_elements) :: vec2 Second expression vector integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_elements Dimension of both vectors real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: result Output scalar distance integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/euclidean_distance.html"},{"title":"distance_to_centroid – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine distance_to_centroid(n_genes, n_families, genes, centroids, gene_to_fam, distances, n_tissues, ierr) For each gene, extracts its expression vector and the centroid of its assigned family, then computes the Euclidean distance between them. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families Total number of gene families real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_genes) :: genes Gene expression matrix (n_tissues × n_genes), column-major real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_families) :: centroids Family centroid matrix (n_tissues × n_families), column-major, -1.0_real64 for unassigned genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_fam Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in genes , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_genes) :: distances Output distances array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_tissues Expression vector dimension integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/distance_to_centroid.html"},{"title":"get_sorted_value – TensorOmics","text":"public  function get_sorted_value(values, sorted_indices, position, ierr) result(sorted_value) Get the value at the sorted position. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: values (:) Input real array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: sorted_indices (:) Permutation index array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: position Sorted position (1-based) integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code Return Value real(kind=real64)","tags":"","url":"proc/get_sorted_value.html"},{"title":"build_bst_index_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine build_bst_index_impl(values, n_values, sorted_indices) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: values (n_values) Input real array to be indexed integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_values Number of elements in values array integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: sorted_indices (n_values) Output permutation index","tags":"","url":"proc/build_bst_index_impl.html"},{"title":"bst_range_query_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine bst_range_query_impl(values, values_perm, n_values, lower_bound, upper_bound, output_indices, n_matches) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: values (n_values) Input real array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: values_perm (n_values) Permutation of values in ascending order -- the BST index. The allocating entry\npoint builds and heapsorts it for you; the expert one takes whatever order you\nsupply, so a caller that already holds one from build_bst_index can reuse it across queries.\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_values . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_values Number of elements real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: lower_bound Lower bound of range (inclusive)\nThe maximum valid value is upper_bound . real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: upper_bound Upper bound of range (inclusive) integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: output_indices (n_values) Output array of matching indices.\nThe first n_matches elements will hold the results. integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_matches Number of matches found","tags":"","url":"proc/bst_range_query_impl.html"},{"title":"compute_shift_vector_field_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_shift_vector_field_c(n_tissues, n_genes, n_families, expression_vectors, family_centroids, gene_to_fam, shift_vectors, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_shift_vector_field_c\") Uses tox_shift_vectors Computes the shift vectors by subtracting the corresponding family centroid from the expression vector. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_tissues Expression vector dimension integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes Total number of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_families Total number of families real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_genes), target :: expression_vectors Gene expression matrix real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_families), target :: family_centroids Family centroid matrix integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_to_fam Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in expression_vectors , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_tissues, 2, n_genes), target :: shift_vectors Output, real matrix array. For each gene it holds two vectors: the centroid of the gene's family first (a zero vector if no family is assigned), then the shift vector integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_shift_vector_field_c.html"},{"title":"loess_smooth_2d_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_smooth_2d_c(n_total, n_target, x_ref, y_ref, indices_used, n_used, x_query, kernel_sigma, kernel_cutoff, y_out, ierr) bind(C, name=\"loess_smooth_2d_c\") Uses f42_stats Smooths y_ref at x_query using reference points x_ref , y_ref , and kernel parameters.\nThe user must pre-filter data and provide only valid indices in indices_used. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_total Total number of reference points. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_target Number of target points to smooth. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_total), target :: x_ref Reference x-coordinates. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_total), target :: y_ref Reference y-coordinates (length n_total). integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_used), target :: indices_used Indices of reference points used for smoothing (only valid indices).\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_total . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_used Number of indices actually used for smoothing. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_target), target :: x_query Target x-coordinates to smooth. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: kernel_sigma Bandwidth parameter for the kernel.\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: kernel_cutoff Cutoff for the kernel, not used if zero\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_target), target :: y_out Output smoothed values (length n_target). integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_smooth_2d_c.html"},{"title":"compute_edf_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_edf_c(values, n_values, unique_values, cdf_values, n_unique, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_edf_c\") Uses f42_stats Returns the sorted unique values and their cumulative frequencies in [0,1].\nThe number of unique values can be determined by finding the last non-zero cdf_value. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_values), target :: values Array of observed data values (e.g., contributions or spikes). integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_values Number of values in the input array. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_values), target :: unique_values Sorted unique data values.\nThe first n_unique elements will hold the results. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_values), target :: cdf_values Corresponding cumulative frequencies between 0 and 1.\nThe first n_unique elements will hold the results. integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_unique Number of unique values found (actual size of output arrays) integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_edf_c.html"},{"title":"compute_edf_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_edf_expert_c(values, n_values, values_perm, unique_values, cdf_values, n_unique, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_edf_expert_c\") Uses f42_stats Returns the sorted unique values and their cumulative frequencies in [0,1].\nThe number of unique values can be determined by finding the last non-zero cdf_value. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_values), target :: values Array of observed data values (e.g., contributions or spikes). integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_values Number of values in the input array. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_values), target :: values_perm Permutation of values in ascending order. The allocating entry point builds\nand heapsorts it for you; the expert one takes whatever order you supply.\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_values . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_values), target :: unique_values Sorted unique data values.\nThe first n_unique elements will hold the results. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_values), target :: cdf_values Corresponding cumulative frequencies between 0 and 1.\nThe first n_unique elements will hold the results. integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_unique Number of unique values found (actual size of output arrays) integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_edf_expert_c.html"},{"title":"calc_percentile_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine calc_percentile_c(array, n_array, percentile, value, n_considered, ierr) bind(C, name=\"calc_percentile_c\") Uses f42_stats Uses linear interpolation between adjacent values. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_array), target :: array input array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_array number of elements in array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: percentile desired percentile as a fraction in [0,1] (e.g. 0.95 for the 95th percentile)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: value output percentile value integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_considered How many leading entries of array_perm the percentile is taken over, for a\npercentile of a subset -- the trailing entries are ignored rather than sliced\noff, so the permutation stays the shape the sort produced. Zero, the default,\nconsiders all n_array of them.\nThe default value is 0_int32 .\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_array . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/calc_percentile_c.html"},{"title":"calc_percentile_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine calc_percentile_expert_c(array, n_array, array_perm, percentile, value, n_considered, ierr) bind(C, name=\"calc_percentile_expert_c\") Uses f42_stats Uses linear interpolation between adjacent values. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_array), target :: array input array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_array number of elements in array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_array), target :: array_perm Permutation of array in ascending order. The allocating entry point builds and\nheapsorts it for you; the expert one takes whatever order you supply.\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_array . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: percentile desired percentile as a fraction in [0,1] (e.g. 0.95 for the 95th percentile)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: value output percentile value integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_considered How many leading entries of array_perm the percentile is taken over, for a\npercentile of a subset -- the trailing entries are ignored rather than sliced\noff, so the permutation stays the shape the sort produced. Zero, the default,\nconsiders all n_array of them.\nThe default value is 0_int32 .\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_array . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/calc_percentile_expert_c.html"},{"title":"compute_scaled_distance_quantile_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_scaled_distance_quantile_c(n_genes, rdi, sorted_rdi, quantile, c_const, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_scaled_distance_quantile_c\") Uses f42_stats This is NOT a null-hypothesis-testing p-value: each distance is compared against the\nobserved distribution it was drawn from, not an independently generated null distribution.\nIt instead measures how extreme an observed distance is relative to all observed distances. Implements:\nQ(d) = ( #{di in D | di >= d} + c ) / ( |D| + c ) Because distances are non-negative, a one-sided upper-tail quantile is used. Assumptions / preconditions:\n- sorted_rdi(1:n_genes) contains the empirical distribution D.\n- If invalid RDIs exist (negative), they should already be mapped to 0 in the distribution Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes Number of genes being processed. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: rdi empirical distribution D\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: sorted_rdi empirical distribution D with non negative values\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_genes), target :: quantile Output array to store the computed quantile for each gene. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: c_const Constant used in the computation, typically 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_scaled_distance_quantile_c.html"},{"title":"compute_scaled_distance_quantile_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_scaled_distance_quantile_expert_c(n_genes, rdi, sorted_rdi, sorted_rdi_perm, quantile, c_const, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_scaled_distance_quantile_expert_c\") Uses f42_stats This is NOT a null-hypothesis-testing p-value: each distance is compared against the\nobserved distribution it was drawn from, not an independently generated null distribution.\nIt instead measures how extreme an observed distance is relative to all observed distances. Implements:\nQ(d) = ( #{di in D | di >= d} + c ) / ( |D| + c ) Because distances are non-negative, a one-sided upper-tail quantile is used. Assumptions / preconditions:\n- sorted_rdi(1:n_genes) contains the empirical distribution D.\n- If invalid RDIs exist (negative), they should already be mapped to 0 in the distribution Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes Number of genes being processed. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: rdi empirical distribution D\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: sorted_rdi empirical distribution D with non negative values\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: sorted_rdi_perm Permutation of sorted_rdi in ascending order. The allocating entry point builds\nand heapsorts it for you; the expert one takes whatever order you supply.\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_genes), target :: quantile Output array to store the computed quantile for each gene. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: c_const Constant used in the computation, typically 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_scaled_distance_quantile_expert_c.html"},{"title":"normalize_variable_timeseries_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine normalize_variable_timeseries_impl(v, v_norm, n_points, status) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: v (n_points) Original time series real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: v_norm (n_points) Normalized time series integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Vector length (number of time points) integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: status Status code for specific warnings","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_variable_timeseries_impl.html"},{"title":"normalize_single_trajectory_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine normalize_single_trajectory_impl(trajectory, trajectory_norm, n_factors, n_timepoints, status) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: trajectory (n_timepoints,n_factors) Original trajectory for one sample real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: trajectory_norm (n_timepoints,n_factors) Normalized trajectory for one sample integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_factors Number of factors/variables integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_timepoints Number of time points integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_factors) :: status Status code for specific warnings, one per factor","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_single_trajectory_impl.html"},{"title":"normalize_all_trajectories_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine normalize_all_trajectories_impl(trajectories, trajectories_norm, n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints, tmp_series, tmp_series_norm, status) independently across time for each sample. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: trajectories (n_factors,n_samples,n_timepoints) Original trajectories real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: trajectories_norm (n_factors,n_samples,n_timepoints) Normalized trajectories integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_factors Number of factors integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_samples Number of samples/entities integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_timepoints Number of time points real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: tmp_series (n_timepoints) Work array: one factor's time series, gathered contiguously real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: tmp_series_norm (n_timepoints) Work array: the normalized time series integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_factors, n_samples) :: status Status code for specific warnings, one per factor per sample","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_all_trajectories_impl.html"},{"title":"euclidean_distance_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine euclidean_distance_c(vec1, vec2, n_elements, result, ierr) bind(C, name=\"euclidean_distance_c\") Uses tox_euclidean_distance Calculates the L2 norm: result = sqrt(sum((vec1_i - vec2_i)**2)) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_elements), target :: vec1 First expression vector real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_elements), target :: vec2 Second expression vector integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_elements Dimension of both vectors real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: result Output scalar distance integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/euclidean_distance_c.html"},{"title":"distance_to_centroid_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine distance_to_centroid_c(n_genes, n_families, genes, centroids, gene_to_fam, distances, n_tissues, ierr) bind(C, name=\"distance_to_centroid_c\") Uses tox_euclidean_distance For each gene, extracts its expression vector and the centroid of its assigned family, then computes the Euclidean distance between them. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes Total number of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_families Total number of gene families real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_genes), target :: genes Gene expression matrix (n_tissues × n_genes), column-major real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_tissues, n_families), target :: centroids Family centroid matrix (n_tissues × n_families), column-major, -1.0_real64 for unassigned genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_to_fam Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in genes , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_genes), target :: distances Output distances array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_tissues Expression vector dimension integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/distance_to_centroid_c.html"},{"title":"build_kd_index_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine build_kd_index_impl(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, tmp_workspace, tmp_value_buffer, tmp_permutation, tmp_recursion_stack) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points) :: points Data points integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions) :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_workspace Workspace array real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_value_buffer Workspace for sorting integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_permutation Workspace for sorting integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(3, n_points) :: tmp_recursion_stack Stack for l, r, depth","tags":"","url":"proc/build_kd_index_impl.html"},{"title":"partial_sort_by_dimension_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine partial_sort_by_dimension_helper(points, n_points, n_dimensions, kd_indices, left_idx, right_idx, dim, tmp_workspace, tmp_value_buffer, tmp_permutation) Sorts kd_indices(left_idx:right_idx) by points(dimension, kd_indices(:)). Internal to the\nindex build, which has already validated everything this reads. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points) :: points Input points array integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points size of points integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(:) :: kd_indices Index array to modify integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: left_idx Left index of subarray integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: right_idx Right index of subarray integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: dim Dimension to sort by integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(:) :: tmp_workspace Workspace array real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(:) :: tmp_value_buffer Buffer for dimension values integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(:) :: tmp_permutation Permutation array","tags":"","url":"proc/partial_sort_by_dimension_helper.html"},{"title":"build_spherical_kd_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine build_spherical_kd_impl(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, tmp_workspace, tmp_value_buffer, tmp_permutation, tmp_recursion_stack) This is a thin, semantically-named wrapper: partitioning is identical to build_kd_index_impl (plain per-axis median splits);\ncallers are responsible for ensuring points are actually unit-normalized beforehand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points) :: points Data points integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions) :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_workspace Workspace array real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_value_buffer Workspace for sorting integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_permutation Workspace for sorting integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(3, n_points) :: tmp_recursion_stack Stack for l, r, depth","tags":"","url":"proc/build_spherical_kd_impl.html"},{"title":"get_kd_point – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine get_kd_point(points, kd_indices, position, point_values, ierr) Retrieves the coordinate vector of the point stored at a given position of a built k-d\nindex, i.e. point_values = points(:, kd_indices(position)) . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(:, :) :: points Input points integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(:) :: kd_indices KD index array\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is size(points, dim=2, kind=int32) . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: position Position in index\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is size(kd_indices, kind=int32) . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(size(points, dim=1, kind=int32)) :: point_values Output point values integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/get_kd_point.html"},{"title":"compute_tissue_versatility_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine compute_tissue_versatility_impl(n_axes, n_vectors, expression_vectors, vectors_selection_mask, n_selected_vectors, axes_selection_mask, n_selected_axes, tissue_versatilities, tissue_angles_deg) The metric is based on the angle between each gene expression vector and the space diagonal.\nVersatility is normalized to [0, 1], where 0 means uniform expression and 1 means expression in only one axis. The masks follow the n_selected_ convention, so the generated wrapper validates that each\nselection count matches its mask; n_selected_axes (not an array extent) carries its own floor. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions) integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_vectors Number of expression vectors (genes) real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_vectors) :: expression_vectors 2D array (n_axes, n_vectors), each column is a gene expression vector logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_vectors) :: vectors_selection_mask Logical array (n_vectors), .TRUE. for vectors to process integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_selected_vectors Number of selected expression vectors (count of .TRUE. in vectors_selection_mask) logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_axes) :: axes_selection_mask Logical array (n_axes), .TRUE. for axes to include in calculation integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_selected_axes Number of selected axes (count of .TRUE. in axes_selection_mask)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_selected_vectors) :: tissue_versatilities Output, real array, length = n_selected_vectors, stores the calculated tissue versatilities real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_selected_vectors) :: tissue_angles_deg Output, real array, length = n_selected_vectors, stores the calculated angles in degrees","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_tissue_versatility_impl.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_helper(arr, n_strings, arr_shape, filename, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(n_strings) :: arr Array to be serialized integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_strings Number of strings in arr integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(:) :: arr_shape Extents of arr , one per dimension character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"C-binding","url":"proc/serialize_char_helper.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_1d – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_1d(arr, filename, ierr) Directly serialize a 1D character array into a file Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(:), contiguous :: arr Array to be serialized character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/serialize_char_1d.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_2d – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_2d(arr, filename, ierr) Directly serialize a 2D character array into a file Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(:, :), contiguous :: arr Array to be serialized character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/serialize_char_2d.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_3d – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_3d(arr, filename, ierr) Directly serialize a 3D character array into a file Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(:, :, :), contiguous :: arr Array to be serialized character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/serialize_char_3d.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_4d – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_4d(arr, filename, ierr) Directly serialize a 4D character array into a file Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(:, :, :, :), contiguous :: arr Array to be serialized character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/serialize_char_4d.html"},{"title":"serialize_char_5d – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine serialize_char_5d(arr, filename, ierr) Directly serialize a 5D character array into a file Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in), dimension(:, :, :, :, :), contiguous :: arr Array to be serialized character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename Name of the file to write to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/serialize_char_5d.html"},{"title":"gjct_permutation_test_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine gjct_permutation_test_impl(neighborhood_residuals_S1, neighborhood_residuals_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points, global_jsd_observed, n_bins, shared_residual_range, n_permutations, jsd_null, p_value, tmp_residuals_S1, tmp_residuals_S2, tmp_pool, tmp_pmf_S1, tmp_pmf_S2, tmp_counts, tmp_included_n_reps_S1, tmp_included_n_reps_S2, tmp_js_divergences, tmp_weights, random_seed, neighbor_mask_S1, neighbor_mask_S2) Tests the null hypothesis that both studies are exchangeable. The residuals are shuffled in\nthe work copies, so the caller's own arrays are left untouched. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S1 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 1, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S2 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 2, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points in the studies real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: global_jsd_observed Observed global JSD value for both studies integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_bins Number of equally sized histogram bins used for the studies real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: shared_residual_range Computed residual range for both studies\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_permutations Number of permutations to perform real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_permutations) :: jsd_null Vector of global divergence values obtained under the null hypothesis real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: p_value Empirical p-value of the permutation test: real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: tmp_residuals_S1 Work copy of study 1's residuals, shuffled in place real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: tmp_residuals_S2 Work copy of study 2's residuals, shuffled in place real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S1 + n_reps_S2, n_neighbors), target :: tmp_pool Working array for shuffling the concatenated residuals from both studies per reference point real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_pmf_S1 Working array for study 1's normalized histogram counts real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_pmf_S2 Working array for study 2's normalized histogram counts integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_counts Working array for the histogram counts integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_included_n_reps_S1 Working array for study 1's included replicate counts integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_included_n_reps_S2 Working array for study 2's included replicate counts real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_js_divergences Working array for the per-reference-point divergences real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: tmp_weights Working array for the divergence weights integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional :: random_seed Seed to use for shuffling logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S1 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 1 (e.g. for family-wise analysis) logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S2 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 2 (e.g. for family-wise analysis)","tags":"","url":"proc/gjct_permutation_test_impl.html"},{"title":"shuffle_reference_point_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine shuffle_reference_point_helper(reference_point_S1, reference_point_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, pool_flat) Internal helper of gjct_permutation_test_impl . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(inout), dimension(n_reps_S1*n_neighbors) :: reference_point_S1 Residuals for one reference point in study 1, will be shuffled in-place real(kind=real64), intent(inout), dimension(n_reps_S2*n_neighbors) :: reference_point_S2 Residuals for one reference point in study 2, will be shuffled in-place integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension((n_reps_S1 + n_reps_S2)*n_neighbors) :: pool_flat Working array for shuffling the concatenated residuals from both studies per reference point","tags":"","url":"proc/shuffle_reference_point_helper.html"},{"title":"omics_vector_RAP_projection_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine omics_vector_RAP_projection_c(vecs, n_axes, n_vecs, vecs_selection_mask, n_selected_vecs, axes_selection_mask, n_selected_axes, projections, ierr) bind(C, name=\"omics_vector_RAP_projection_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_vecs), target :: vecs matrix with expression vectors integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes number of axes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_vecs number of vectors per axis logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_vecs), target :: vecs_selection_mask .true. for vectors where projection is to be computed integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_selected_vecs count of .true. values in vecs_selection_mask logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: axes_selection_mask .true. for axes to be included in RAP integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_selected_axes count of .true. values in axes_selection_mask real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_selected_axes, n_selected_vecs), target :: projections projected vectors integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/omics_vector_rap_projection_c.html"},{"title":"omics_field_RAP_projection_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine omics_field_RAP_projection_c(fields, n_axes, n_fields, fields_selection_mask, n_selected_fields, axes_selection_mask, n_selected_axes, projections, ierr) bind(C, name=\"omics_field_RAP_projection_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, 2, n_fields), target :: fields matrix with vector fields; each field holds two vectors, the origin first and the target second integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes number of axes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_fields number of vectors per axis logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_fields), target :: fields_selection_mask .true. for vectors where projection is to be computed integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_selected_fields count of .true. values in fields_selection_mask logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: axes_selection_mask .true. for axes to be included in RAP integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_selected_axes count of .true. values in axes_selection_mask real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_selected_axes, n_selected_fields), target :: projections projected vectors integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/omics_field_rap_projection_c.html"},{"title":"clock_hand_angle_between_vectors_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine clock_hand_angle_between_vectors_c(v1, v2, n_dims, orientation_reference, signed_angle, ierr) bind(C, name=\"clock_hand_angle_between_vectors_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools The unsigned angle is acos(v1 . v2) ; orientation_reference supplies the sign by saying\nwhich way round the plane the two vectors span counts as positive. Reports ERR_INVALID_INPUT when the reference is orthogonal to the rotation and so orients nothing. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: v1 First normalized vector in RAP space real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: v2 Second normalized vector in RAP space integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims Dimension of both vectors real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: orientation_reference Orients the plane the rotation happens in, so the angle can carry a sign. A\nrotation from one vector to another has no inherent direction above two\ndimensions -- and in RAP space not even in two, since the axes are tissues or\nfactors and carry no handedness -- so the caller states which way round counts\nas positive. The sign is that of this vector's component along the rotation. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: signed_angle Signed angle between vectors in radians [-pi, pi] integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/clock_hand_angle_between_vectors_c.html"},{"title":"clock_hand_angles_for_shift_vectors_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine clock_hand_angles_for_shift_vectors_c(fields, n_dims, n_fields, fields_selection_mask, n_selected_fields, orientation_reference, signed_angles, ierr) bind(C, name=\"clock_hand_angles_for_shift_vectors_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Each selected field is angled by the rule of clock_hand_angle_between_vectors_impl ,\nwith one orientation_reference shared by the whole batch. A single field whose rotation\nthe reference fails to orient fails the call. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, 2, n_fields), target :: fields matrix with vector fields; each field holds two vectors, the origin first and the target second integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims Dimension of each vector in RAP space integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_fields Number of vector pairs logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_fields), target :: fields_selection_mask .true. for vector pairs where angle should be computed integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_selected_fields Count of .true. values in fields_selection_mask real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: orientation_reference Orients the plane the rotation happens in, so the angle can carry a sign. A\nrotation from one vector to another has no inherent direction above two\ndimensions -- and in RAP space not even in two, since the axes are tissues or\nfactors and carry no handedness -- so the caller states which way round counts\nas positive. The sign is that of this vector's component along the rotation. real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_selected_fields), target :: signed_angles Signed rotation angles between vector pairs in radians [-π, π] integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/clock_hand_angles_for_shift_vectors_c.html"},{"title":"compute_relative_axis_contributions_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine compute_relative_axis_contributions_c(vec, n_axes, contributions, ierr) bind(C, name=\"compute_relative_axis_contributions_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Shared utility: the shift-vector and expression-vector entry points below both drive it. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: vec RAP-projected and normalized vector (expression or shift) integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes Number of axes (length of vec and contributions) real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_axes), target :: contributions Fractional contribution of each axis (output), values in [0,1], sum to 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_relative_axis_contributions_c.html"},{"title":"relative_axes_changes_from_shift_vector_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine relative_axes_changes_from_shift_vector_c(vec, n_axes, contributions, ierr) bind(C, name=\"relative_axes_changes_from_shift_vector_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Wrapper for shift vectors (e.g. difference between two RAP-projected vectors) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: vec RAP-projected and normalized shift vector integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes Number of axes real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_axes), target :: contributions Fractional contribution of each axis (output), values in [0,1], sum to 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/relative_axes_changes_from_shift_vector_c.html"},{"title":"relative_axes_expression_from_expression_vector_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine relative_axes_expression_from_expression_vector_c(vec, n_axes, contributions, ierr) bind(C, name=\"relative_axes_expression_from_expression_vector_c\") Uses tox_relative_axis_plane_tools Wrapper for single RAP-projected expression vectors Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: vec RAP-projected and normalized expression vector integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes Number of axes real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_axes), target :: contributions Fractional contribution of each axis (output), values in [0,1], sum to 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/relative_axes_expression_from_expression_vector_c.html"},{"title":"mean_vector – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine mean_vector(expression_vectors, n_axes, n_genes, gene_indices, n_selected_genes, centroid, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: expression_vectors The input matrix of all gene expression vectors (n_axes x n_genes). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes in the input matrix. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_selected_genes) :: gene_indices An array containing the column indices of the selected genes in 'expression_vectors'.\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_selected_genes The number of genes in the current family to be averaged.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_axes) :: centroid The output vector representing the computed centroid. integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/mean_vector.html"},{"title":"group_centroid_orthologs – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine group_centroid_orthologs(expression_vectors, n_axes, n_genes, gene_to_family, n_families, centroid_matrix, ortholog_set, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: expression_vectors The input matrix of all gene expression vectors (n_axes x n_genes). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes in the 'expression_vectors' matrix. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_family Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in expression_vectors , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families Total number of gene families to compute centroids for. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_axes, n_families) :: centroid_matrix The output matrix (n_axes x n_families) to store the computed centroids. logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: ortholog_set A logical array indicating if a gene is part of a specific subset (e.g., orthologs). integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/group_centroid_orthologs.html"},{"title":"group_centroid_orthologs_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine group_centroid_orthologs_expert(expression_vectors, n_axes, n_genes, gene_to_family, n_families, centroid_matrix, tmp_group_indices, ortholog_set, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: expression_vectors The input matrix of all gene expression vectors (n_axes x n_genes). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes in the 'expression_vectors' matrix. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_family Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in expression_vectors , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families Total number of gene families to compute centroids for. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_axes, n_families) :: centroid_matrix The output matrix (n_axes x n_families) to store the computed centroids. integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_genes) :: tmp_group_indices Work array for storing the indices of one family's genes. logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: ortholog_set A logical array indicating if a gene is part of a specific subset (e.g., orthologs). integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/group_centroid_orthologs_expert.html"},{"title":"group_centroid_all – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine group_centroid_all(expression_vectors, n_axes, n_genes, gene_to_family, n_families, centroid_matrix, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: expression_vectors The input matrix of all gene expression vectors (n_axes x n_genes). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes in the 'expression_vectors' matrix. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_family Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in expression_vectors , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families Total number of gene families to compute centroids for. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_axes, n_families) :: centroid_matrix The output matrix (n_axes x n_families) to store the computed centroids. integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/group_centroid_all.html"},{"title":"group_centroid_all_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine group_centroid_all_expert(expression_vectors, n_axes, n_genes, gene_to_family, n_families, centroid_matrix, tmp_group_indices, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: expression_vectors The input matrix of all gene expression vectors (n_axes x n_genes). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes Number of axes (tissues/dimensions). integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Total number of genes in the 'expression_vectors' matrix. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_family Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in expression_vectors , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families Total number of gene families to compute centroids for. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_axes, n_families) :: centroid_matrix The output matrix (n_axes x n_families) to store the computed centroids. integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_genes) :: tmp_group_indices Work array for storing the indices of one family's genes. integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/group_centroid_all_expert.html"},{"title":"mask_get_first_successor_idx – TensorOmics","text":"public pure function mask_get_first_successor_idx(bit_mask) result(idx) Helper function that returns the index after the last active gene in bit_mask , so the first succeeding gene. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(:) :: bit_mask chunked mask to mark active genes Return Value integer(kind=int32) index of last active gene","tags":"","url":"proc/mask_get_first_successor_idx.html"},{"title":"mask_check_state – TensorOmics","text":"public pure function mask_check_state(bit_mask, i_gene) result(state) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(:) :: bit_mask chunked mask to mark active paralogs integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: i_gene index of paralog to be marked active Return Value logical check result","tags":"C-binding","url":"proc/mask_check_state.html"},{"title":"detect_neofunctionalization_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine detect_neofunctionalization_impl(ancestors, n_families, genes, n_axes, gene_to_fam, n_genes, thresholds, neofunc) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_families) :: ancestors RAP projected unit length expression vector of ancestral ortholog\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families number of vectors in ancestors real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes) :: genes RAP projected unit length expression vectors of genes\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_axes size of ancestors vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_fam Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in genes , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of vectors in genes real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_axes) :: thresholds threshold per axis that defines significant change in expression, may be a percentile of all genes' changes per axis\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(out), dimension(n_genes, n_axes) :: neofunc .true. if neofunctionalization has been detected for the respective axes, always .false. for unassigned genes","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_neofunctionalization_impl.html"},{"title":"detect_patterns_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine detect_patterns_impl(ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, pattern_mode, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, n_results, max_subset_size, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, tmp_active_mask, tmp_paralog_vector, max_angle, gain_gamma, rdi_threshold, paralog_norms, sorted_paralog_norms_perm, tmp_work_array, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dims) :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes) :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: pattern_mode used pattern for detection Mode Value Procedure Dosage Effect MODE_DOSAGE_PATTERN detect_dosage_effect Subfunctionalization MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN detect_subfunctionalization integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask with the genes' indices kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0. Build it with the matching filter_paralogs_by_pattern_* routine integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes. Use subroutine mask_chunk_count for calculation\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_results number of resulting subsets. They are stored as the first n_results elements of work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: max_subset_size maximum subset size of checked gene subsets. Too large a value is capped to the\nmaximum valid size. The bindings cap it automatically while sizing the work\narray; a Fortran caller caps it by calling calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size first.\nZero is in range and means there is no subset to check -- the sizing routine reports\nit whenever the filtered families hold a single gene each. It reports a work array\nof zero slots along with it, which this routine does not accept, so a caller that\ngets zero back has nothing to detect and should not call here at all.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets) :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. Note Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets .\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the work_array_size output produced by calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: tmp_active_mask working array to hold the extended subsets real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_dims) :: tmp_paralog_vector vector used for pruning subsets real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: max_angle maximum angle in radians 0<=angle<=Pi that a subset candidate must not exceed, otherwise pruned\nThis optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_DOSAGE_PATTERN .\nThe default value is 4.0_real64*atan(1.0_real64) .\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: gain_gamma positive magnitude gain for dosage effect\nThis optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_DOSAGE_PATTERN .\nThe default value is 0.1_real64 .\nThe minimum valid value is above(0.0_real64) . real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: rdi_threshold max allowed residual distance from ancestor This optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN .\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: paralog_norms euclidean norms of the genes, used for subset pruning ( norm from f42_vector_impl computes them)\nThis optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN .\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: sorted_paralog_norms_perm ascending permutation of the norms, for subset pruning: the smallest norm among the genes that could extend a subset must not fall below the subset's angle to the ancestor\nThis optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN .\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . real(kind=real64), intent(out), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: tmp_work_array work array for checking the minimum value after a given index efficiently\nThis optional argument needs to be passed if used mode ( pattern_mode ) is MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN . integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_patterns_impl.html"},{"title":"generate_subsets_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine generate_subsets_helper(candidate_mask, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, pattern, ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, tmp_paralog_vector, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, n_results, n_active_masks, n_new_active_masks, dosage_max_angle, dosage_gain_gamma, subfunc_rdi_threshold, subfunc_paralog_norms, subfunc_sorted_paralog_norms_perm, tmp_subfunc_work_array, ierr) Uses f42_math_impl Generates subsets for candidate_mask by extending it with one valid gene.\nSubsets with bad gene constellation will be pruned,\nothers will be added to the work array, either as new active subset that will be extended in coming iterations or as result. Doesn't do any input validation.\nprepare sum vector, so the extending gene just needs to be included in one operation and excluded after calculation initialize work array with min values, so each index i holds the min value in subarray subfunc_paralog_norms(i:n_genes)\nalso, prepare residual, so the extending gene just needs to be included in one operation and excluded after calculation Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(inout), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: candidate_mask working array to hold a subset that is a potential result candidate integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask that will have indices of genes kept by pattern set to 1, else 0 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: pattern used pattern for detection Mode Value Dosage Effect MODE_DOSAGE_PATTERN Subfunctionalization MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dims) :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes) :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes real(kind=real64), intent(inout), dimension(n_dims) :: tmp_paralog_vector vector used for pruning subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets) :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets . USE calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size TO DETERMINE THIS NUMBER integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: n_results number of results in work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_active_masks number of active subsets in work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: n_new_active_masks number of new active subsets in work_arr_paralog_subsets real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: dosage_max_angle maximum angle in radians that a subset candidate must not exceed, otherwise pruned, default is Pi real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: dosage_gain_gamma positive magnitude gain for dosage, default 0.1 real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional :: subfunc_rdi_threshold max allowed residual distance from ancestor real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: subfunc_paralog_norms euclidean norms of the genes, used for subset pruning ( norm from f42_vector_impl computes them) integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: subfunc_sorted_paralog_norms_perm ascending permutation of the norms, for subset pruning: the smallest norm among the genes that could extend a subset must not fall below the subset's angle to the ancestor real(kind=real64), intent(out), optional, dimension(n_genes) :: tmp_subfunc_work_array work array for checking the minimum value after a given index efficiently integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/generate_subsets_helper.html"},{"title":"fill_array_with_minvals_for_each_idx – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine fill_array_with_minvals_for_each_idx(out_arr, src_arr, sorted_src_arr_perm, src_arr_len, ierr) Helper for subfunctionalization pruning. It initializes a working array with each index i holding the min value in subarray src_arr(i:src_arr_len) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(out), optional, dimension(src_arr_len) :: out_arr output array, e.g. source [50, 75, 0, 100, 25] would lead to out_arr [0, 0, 0, 25, 25] real(kind=real64), intent(in), optional, dimension(src_arr_len) :: src_arr array that holds the original values integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional, dimension(src_arr_len) :: sorted_src_arr_perm sorted permutation vector, so each index holds the index of the value in src_arr as if src_arr was soted ascending integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: src_arr_len number elements in src_arr integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/fill_array_with_minvals_for_each_idx.html"},{"title":"take_active_mask_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine take_active_mask_helper(subsets, n_mask_chunks, n_subsets, n_results, n_active_masks, n_new_active_masks, active_mask, ierr) For memory efficiency this subroutine helps holding different kinds of masks in a single array.\nTo achieve this, subsets has this structure: [...results, ...active_masks, ...new_active_masks]\nThis routine removes an active mask from the subsets array and returns it in active_mask . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(inout), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_subsets) :: subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks in a mask integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_subsets number of subsets that can be hold integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_results number of results in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: n_active_masks number of active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_new_active_masks number of new active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: active_mask taken active mask from subsets , will be remove from subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/take_active_mask_helper.html"},{"title":"add_to_results_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine add_to_results_helper(subsets, n_mask_chunks, n_subsets, n_results, n_active_masks, n_new_active_masks, result, ierr) For memory efficiency this subroutine helps holding different kinds of masks in a single array.\nTo achieve this, subsets has this structure: [...results, ...active_masks, ...new_active_masks]\nThis routine adds a result mask to subsets array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(inout), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_subsets) :: subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks in a mask integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_subsets number of subsets that can be hold integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: n_results number of results in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_active_masks number of active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_new_active_masks number of new active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: result result to add to subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/add_to_results_helper.html"},{"title":"add_new_active_mask_helper – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine add_new_active_mask_helper(subsets, n_mask_chunks, n_subsets, n_results, n_active_masks, n_new_active_masks, new_active_mask, ierr) For memory efficiency this subroutine helps holding different kinds of masks in a single array.\nTo achieve this, subsets has this structure: [...results, ...active_masks, ...new_active_masks]\nThis routine adds a new active mask to the subsets array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_subsets) :: subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks in a mask integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_subsets number of subsets that can be hold integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_results number of results in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_active_masks number of active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: n_new_active_masks number of new active masks in subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: new_active_mask new active mask to add to subsets integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/add_new_active_mask_helper.html"},{"title":"mask_chunk_count – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine mask_chunk_count(n_genes, count) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: count number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks","tags":"C-binding","url":"proc/mask_chunk_count.html"},{"title":"filter_paralogs_by_pattern_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine filter_paralogs_by_pattern_impl(pattern_mode, gene_angles, threshold, n_genes, n_families, gene_to_fam, masks, n_mask_chunks, ierr) This subroutine prefilters the genes for a specific pattern to reduce detection overhead, as less subsets need to be tried. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: pattern_mode used pattern for detection Mode Value Procedure Dosage Effect MODE_DOSAGE_PATTERN filter_paralogs_by_pattern_dosage_effect Subfunctionalization MODE_SUBFUNC_PATTERN filter_paralogs_by_pattern_subfunctionalization real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_angles vector, holding the angles between ancestor and genes (0<=angle<=Pi)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: threshold filter threshold integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_families number of families\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: gene_to_fam a mapping of gene index to family index, so gene i is related to gene_angles(i) and part of family j=gene_to_fam(i) .\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families . integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_families) :: masks bit mask that will have the indices of genes kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/filter_paralogs_by_pattern_impl.html"},{"title":"calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size(max_subset_size, n_genes, work_array_size, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, ierr) The detect_* subroutines need a work array for the to be tested subsets.\nIn worst case, all need to be tried and subsets that cannot be extended will be kept as results.\nThis is the reason why the work array holds the results as well, as all subsets that are stored in the array can be results as well. This subroutine calculates the needed size for the work array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(inout) :: max_subset_size maximum size that a subset must not exceed. Zero is in range and means there is\nnothing to size a work array for, which is reported back as a size of zero.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . Warning If the desired size is too large and leads to an integer overflow, max_subset_size will be set to the maximum valid size. Also, size will be set to number of genes in filtered_paralogs_mask if larger. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes number of genes integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: work_array_size The calculated needed work array size in absolute worst case scenario. Look into source for details. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks) :: filtered_paralogs_mask Output mask with all genes disabled that did not pass the filter integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"C-binding","url":"proc/calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size.html"},{"title":"mask_set_state – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine mask_set_state(bit_mask, i_gene, state, ierr) Sets the state of a bit/gene in bit_mask Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(:) :: bit_mask chunked mask to mark active paralogs integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: i_gene index of paralog to be marked active logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in) :: state state the bit should be set to integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/mask_set_state.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_plain_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_plain_c(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, fitted_values, ierr) bind(C, name=\"loess_fit_plain_c\") Uses tox_loess Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using the specified smoothing parameter and outputs the smoothed\nresponse array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n Total number of data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: y Response variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n, 1), target :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_plain_c.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_plain_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_plain_expert_c(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, tmp_int_workspace, int_workspace_size, tmp_real_workspace, real_workspace_size, tmp_hat_diag, fitted_values, ierr) bind(C, name=\"loess_fit_plain_expert_c\") Uses tox_loess Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using the specified smoothing parameter and outputs the smoothed\nresponse array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n Total number of data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: y Response variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n, 1), target :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(int_workspace_size), target :: tmp_int_workspace Integer workspace array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: int_workspace_size Required size of the integer workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the int_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 10000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(real_workspace_size), target :: tmp_real_workspace Real workspace array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: real_workspace_size Required size of the real workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the real_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 100000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_hat_diag Diagonal elements of the hat matrix real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_plain_expert_c.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_robust_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_robust_c(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, n_iters, fitted_values, ierr) bind(C, name=\"loess_fit_robust_c\") Uses tox_loess Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using robust iterations to handle outliers.\nThe robust fitting process iterates n_iters times, each iteration:\n- Combines original weights with robust weights (down-weights from previous iteration)\n- Runs LOESS fitting with combined weights\n- Computes residuals (y - fitted values)\n- Updates robust weights using bisquare function (suppresses large residuals) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n Total number of data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: y Response variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n, 1), target :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_iters Number of robust iterations\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe default value is 3_int32 . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_robust_c.html"},{"title":"loess_fit_robust_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine loess_fit_robust_expert_c(n, x, y, weights, eval_points, span, degree, max_neighborhood_size, compute_influence, save_factorization, n_iters, tmp_int_workspace, int_workspace_size, tmp_real_workspace, real_workspace_size, tmp_hat_diag, tmp_robust_weights, tmp_combined_weights, tmp_residuals, tmp_permutation_indices, fitted_values, ierr) bind(C, name=\"loess_fit_robust_expert_c\") Uses tox_loess Data too degenerate to fit is answered directly, by the observations themselves; see loess_degenerate_fit .\nFits a LOESS model to the data using robust iterations to handle outliers.\nThe robust fitting process iterates n_iters times, each iteration:\n- Combines original weights with robust weights (down-weights from previous iteration)\n- Runs LOESS fitting with combined weights\n- Computes residuals (y - fitted values)\n- Updates robust weights using bisquare function (suppresses large residuals) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n Total number of data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: x Predictor variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: y Response variable array real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n), target :: weights Weight array for data points real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n, 1), target :: eval_points Evaluation points (x values at which the fitted curve is computed) real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: span Smoothing parameter for LOESS\nThe minimum valid value is EPS_LOESS .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: degree Degree of the LOESS polynomial\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_neighborhood_size Maximum neighborhood size logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: compute_influence Influence calculation flag\nThe default value is .false. . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), target :: save_factorization Save matrix factorization flag\nThe default value is .false. . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_iters Number of robust iterations\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe default value is 3_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(int_workspace_size), target :: tmp_int_workspace Integer workspace array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: int_workspace_size Required size of the integer workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the int_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 10000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(real_workspace_size), target :: tmp_real_workspace Real workspace array integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: real_workspace_size Required size of the real workspace array\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the real_workspace_size output produced by tox_loess_required_workspace .\nThe minimum valid value is 100000_int32 . Producer input Supplied by n_dim 1_int32 real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_hat_diag Diagonal elements of the hat matrix real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_robust_weights Robust bisquare weights (updated each iteration, initialized to 1.0) real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_combined_weights Combined weights: product of user weights and robust weights (weights(i) * robust_weights(i)) real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_residuals Residuals (y - fitted_values), used to compute bisquare robust weights integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: tmp_permutation_indices Permutation indices array (from NetLib bisquare weight computation) real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n), target :: fitted_values Fitted (smoothed) values of y at the evaluation points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/loess_fit_robust_expert_c.html"},{"title":"cluster_factor_trajectories_k_means_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine cluster_factor_trajectories_k_means_c(n_clusters, trajectories, n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints, centroids, labels, label_counts, max_iterations, ierr) bind(C, name=\"cluster_factor_trajectories_k_means_c\") Uses tox_clustering Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_clusters number ( k ) of clusters\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_samples*n_timepoints . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints), target :: trajectories matrix with data points to cluster integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_factors number of factors integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_samples number of samples integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_timepoints number of timepoints real(kind=c_double), intent(inout), dimension(n_factors, n_clusters), target :: centroids matrix with initial centroids of the clusters, could be random data or actual points or unassigned garbage.\nThe centroids should be unique. This is not checked in this routine. The final values will be the final centroids of the clusters integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_samples*n_timepoints), target :: labels array of labels, each index corresponds to the respective point's index, so first label is first point's label. each label is the index of its related cluster -> 1<=label<=n_clusters=k integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_clusters), target :: label_counts holds the number of points having the respective label assigned integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_iterations number of maximum iterations of the clustering integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/cluster_factor_trajectories_k_means_c.html"},{"title":"k_means_clustering_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine k_means_clustering_c(n_clusters, data_points, n_points, n_dims, centroids, labels, label_counts, max_iterations, ierr) bind(C, name=\"k_means_clustering_c\") Uses tox_clustering Assigns each data point to one of k clusters whose centroid is clostest Recalculates the centroids using the mean of its assigned points repeat 1-2 until assignment remains unchanged Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_clusters number ( k ) of clusters\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_points . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_points), target :: data_points matrix with data points to cluster integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points number of points to cluster integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims number of elements a point has real(kind=c_double), intent(inout), dimension(n_dims, n_clusters), target :: centroids matrix with initial centroids of the clusters, could be random data or actual points or unassigned garbage.\nThe centroids should be unique. This is not checked in this routine. The final values will be the final centroids of the clusters integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: labels array of labels, each index corresponds to the respective point's index, so first label is first point's label. each label is the index of its related cluster -> 1<=label<=n_clusters=k integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_clusters), target :: label_counts holds the number of points having the respective label assigned integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_iterations number of maximum iterations of the clustering.\nThe default value is 300_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/k_means_clustering_c.html"},{"title":"linkage_clustering_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine linkage_clustering_c(distances, n_points, merge_i, merge_j, heights, cluster_sizes, method, ierr) bind(C, name=\"linkage_clustering_c\") Uses tox_clustering_impl tox_clustering Note The bottom triangle is used as scratch and restored from the top triangle before\nreturning, on success or on error, so the matrix comes back unchanged. There is no\nneed to copy it before calling. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(inout), dimension(n_points, n_points), target :: distances symmetric distance matrix, holding the positive distances between points. Distance of X->X is always zero. Note The bottom triangle is used as scratch and restored from the top triangle before\nreturning, on success or on error, so the matrix comes back unchanged. There is no\nneed to copy it before calling. Its structure (symmetry, non-negativity, zero diagonal) is validated by the\ndistance-matrix naming convention in the generated wrapper. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points number of points to cluster integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points - 1), target :: merge_i holds cluster labels of the merged node pair at iteration k -> positives relate to leafs/data point indices, negatives to inner nodes integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points - 1), target :: merge_j holds cluster labels of the merged node pair at iteration k -> positives relate to leafs/data point indices, negatives to inner nodes real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points - 1), target :: heights height of the shorter branch of the merge, e.g. if (A,B)+(C) merges to ((A,B),C), the branch to (A,B) is shorter integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points - 1), target :: cluster_sizes size of cluster at iteration k character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in), dimension(8), target :: method used algorithm\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is 2_int32 . Method Value Average / UPGMA METHOD_AVERAGE Weighted / WPGMA METHOD_WEIGHTED Ward METHOD_WARD integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/linkage_clustering_c.html"},{"title":"compute_gene_means – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine compute_gene_means(n_genes, n_reps, expr, means, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Number of genes in the study integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps Number of biological replicates in the study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps, n_genes) :: expr Expression matrix\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_genes) :: means Per-gene mean expression values integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_gene_means.html"},{"title":"compute_residuals – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine compute_residuals(n_genes, n_reps, expr, means, resid, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes Number of genes in the study integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps Number of biological replicates in the study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps, n_genes) :: expr Expression matrix\nNaN is permitted for this value.\nInfinite values are permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes) :: means Per-gene mean expression values; NaN where every replicate of a gene was NaN\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps, n_genes) :: resid Matrix of signed residuals integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_residuals.html"},{"title":"pool_means – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine pool_means(pooled_means, pool_size, n_points, n_pool, x_star, ierr) This takes the pool already built; pool_study_means pools the means of two studies\nfirst, if that is what is at hand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(pool_size) :: pooled_means Pooled means\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: pool_size Number of means in the pool, usually n_genes_S1 + n_genes_S2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points to define integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_pool Total number of included (non-NaN) pooled mean-expression values real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/pool_means.html"},{"title":"pool_means_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine pool_means_expert(pooled_means, pooled_means_perm, pool_size, n_points, n_pool, x_star, ierr) This takes the pool already built; pool_study_means pools the means of two studies\nfirst, if that is what is at hand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(pool_size) :: pooled_means Pooled means\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(pool_size) :: pooled_means_perm Sorting permutation for pooled_means integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: pool_size Number of means in the pool, usually n_genes_S1 + n_genes_S2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points to define integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_pool Total number of included (non-NaN) pooled mean-expression values real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/pool_means_expert.html"},{"title":"pool_study_means – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine pool_study_means(n_genes_S1, mean_S1, n_genes_S2, mean_S2, n_points, n_pool, x_star, ierr) Concatenates the two studies' means, sorts the pool, and turns it into reference\npoints exactly as pool_means does. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S1 Number of genes in study S1 real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S1) :: mean_S1 Per-gene mean expression values of study S1\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S2 Number of genes in study S2 real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S2) :: mean_S2 Per-gene mean expression values of study S2\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points to define integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_pool Total number of included (non-NaN) pooled mean-expression values real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/pool_study_means.html"},{"title":"pool_study_means_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine pool_study_means_expert(n_genes_S1, mean_S1, n_genes_S2, mean_S2, n_points, tmp_pooled_means, tmp_pooled_means_perm, n_pool, x_star, ierr) Concatenates the two studies' means, sorts the pool, and turns it into reference\npoints exactly as pool_means does. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S1 Number of genes in study S1 real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S1) :: mean_S1 Per-gene mean expression values of study S1\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S2 Number of genes in study S2 real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S2) :: mean_S2 Per-gene mean expression values of study S2\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points to define real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_genes_S1+n_genes_S2) :: tmp_pooled_means Work array holding the concatenated means of both studies integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_genes_S1+n_genes_S2) :: tmp_pooled_means_perm Work array for the permutation that sorts tmp_pooled_means integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: n_pool Total number of included (non-NaN) pooled mean-expression values real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/pool_study_means_expert.html"},{"title":"construct_neighborhoods – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine construct_neighborhoods(n_points, x_star, n_genes_S, mean_S, n_reps_S, resid_S, neighborhood_residuals, neighborhood_indices, n_neighbors, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S Number of genes in the current study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S) :: mean_S Per-gene mean expression values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S Number of biological replicates in the study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S, n_genes_S) :: resid_S Matrix of signed residuals\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals Collection of residual vectors for each neighborhood integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_indices Indices of selected neighborhood genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors; a gene whose mean is NaN can never be a neighbor, so this\ncannot exceed the number of genes with a defined mean\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is count(.not. ieee_is_nan(mean_S), kind=int32) .\nIt is recommended to compute this with calc_neighborhood_size . integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/construct_neighborhoods.html"},{"title":"construct_neighborhoods_expert – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine construct_neighborhoods_expert(n_points, x_star, n_genes_S, mean_S, n_reps_S, resid_S, tmp_distances, tmp_distances_perm, neighborhood_residuals, neighborhood_indices, n_neighbors, ierr) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_points) :: x_star Mean-expression reference points\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S Number of genes in the current study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S) :: mean_S Per-gene mean expression values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S Number of biological replicates in the study real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S, n_genes_S) :: resid_S Matrix of signed residuals\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_genes_S) :: tmp_distances Distances work array integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_genes_S) :: tmp_distances_perm Work array for the permutation that sorts tmp_distances real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals Collection of residual vectors for each neighborhood integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_indices Indices of selected neighborhood genes integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors; a gene whose mean is NaN can never be a neighbor, so this\ncannot exceed the number of genes with a defined mean\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is count(.not. ieee_is_nan(mean_S), kind=int32) .\nIt is recommended to compute this with calc_neighborhood_size . integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/construct_neighborhoods_expert.html"},{"title":"gjct_permutation_test_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine gjct_permutation_test_c(neighborhood_residuals_S1, neighborhood_residuals_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points, global_jsd_observed, n_bins, shared_residual_range, n_permutations, jsd_null, p_value, random_seed, neighbor_mask_S1, neighbor_mask_S2, ierr) bind(C, name=\"gjct_permutation_test_c\") Uses tox_data_integration_stats Tests the null hypothesis that both studies are exchangeable. The residuals are shuffled in\nthe work copies, so the caller's own arrays are left untouched. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: neighborhood_residuals_S1 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 1, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: neighborhood_residuals_S2 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 2, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of reference points in the studies real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: global_jsd_observed Observed global JSD value for both studies integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_bins Number of equally sized histogram bins used for the studies real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: shared_residual_range Computed residual range for both studies\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_permutations Number of permutations to perform real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_permutations), target :: jsd_null Vector of global divergence values obtained under the null hypothesis real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: p_value Empirical p-value of the permutation test: integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), optional :: random_seed Seed to use for shuffling logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S1 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 1 (e.g. for family-wise analysis) logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S2 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 2 (e.g. for family-wise analysis) integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/gjct_permutation_test_c.html"},{"title":"gjct_permutation_test_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine gjct_permutation_test_expert_c(neighborhood_residuals_S1, neighborhood_residuals_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points, global_jsd_observed, n_bins, shared_residual_range, n_permutations, jsd_null, p_value, tmp_residuals_S1, tmp_residuals_S2, tmp_pool, tmp_pmf_S1, tmp_pmf_S2, tmp_counts, tmp_included_n_reps_S1, tmp_included_n_reps_S2, tmp_js_divergences, tmp_weights, random_seed, neighbor_mask_S1, neighbor_mask_S2, ierr) bind(C, name=\"gjct_permutation_test_expert_c\") Uses tox_data_integration_stats Tests the null hypothesis that both studies are exchangeable. The residuals are shuffled in\nthe work copies, so the caller's own arrays are left untouched. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: neighborhood_residuals_S1 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 1, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: neighborhood_residuals_S2 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 2, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of reference points in the studies real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: global_jsd_observed Observed global JSD value for both studies integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_bins Number of equally sized histogram bins used for the studies real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: shared_residual_range Computed residual range for both studies\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_permutations Number of permutations to perform real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_permutations), target :: jsd_null Vector of global divergence values obtained under the null hypothesis real(kind=c_double), intent(out), target :: p_value Empirical p-value of the permutation test: real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: tmp_residuals_S1 Work copy of study 1's residuals, shuffled in place real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points), target :: tmp_residuals_S2 Work copy of study 2's residuals, shuffled in place real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_reps_S1 + n_reps_S2, n_neighbors), target :: tmp_pool Working array for shuffling the concatenated residuals from both studies per reference point real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins), target :: tmp_pmf_S1 Working array for study 1's normalized histogram counts real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins), target :: tmp_pmf_S2 Working array for study 2's normalized histogram counts integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins), target :: tmp_counts Working array for the histogram counts integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_included_n_reps_S1 Working array for study 1's included replicate counts integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_included_n_reps_S2 Working array for study 2's included replicate counts real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_js_divergences Working array for the per-reference-point divergences real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_weights Working array for the divergence weights integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), optional :: random_seed Seed to use for shuffling logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S1 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 1 (e.g. for family-wise analysis) logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), optional, dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S2 Optional mask to exclude specific neighbors from study 2 (e.g. for family-wise analysis) integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/gjct_permutation_test_expert_c.html"},{"title":"fjct_compute_jsd_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine fjct_compute_jsd_impl(family_idx, gene_to_family_S1, gene_to_family_S2, n_genes_S1, n_genes_S2, neighborhood_residuals_S1, neighborhood_residuals_S2, neighborhood_genes_S1, neighborhood_genes_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points, n_bins, shared_residual_range, js_divergences, included_n_reps_S1, included_n_reps_S2, total_included_n_reps, global_js_divergence, weights, tmp_neighbor_mask_S1, tmp_neighbor_mask_S2, tmp_pmf_S1, tmp_pmf_S2, tmp_counts) Reuses the same conditioning-on-mean-expression pipeline as the global gJCT, but restricts\nthe residual samples to the genes belonging to the family family_idx . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: family_idx Index of the family that should be analyzed\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S1) :: gene_to_family_S1 Mapping for study 1: Each index (gene) holds the index of its family\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_genes_S2) :: gene_to_family_S2 Mapping for study 2: Each index (gene) holds the index of its family\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S1 Number of genes in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_genes_S2 Number of genes in study 2 real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S1 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 1, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S2 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 2, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_genes_S1 Indices of the selected neighborhood genes of study 1\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes_S1 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_genes_S2 Indices of the selected neighborhood genes of study 2\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes_S2 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points in the studies integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_bins Number of equally sized histogram bins used for the studies real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: shared_residual_range Computed residual range for both studies\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: js_divergences Jensen-Shannon divergence per reference point, computed for studies S1 and S2 integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: included_n_reps_S1 Count of non-NaN residuals (included ones) in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: included_n_reps_S2 Count of non-NaN residuals (included ones) in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: total_included_n_reps Total number of included replicates from both studies ( ) real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: global_js_divergence Weighted global Jensen-Shannon divergence real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: weights Weights used for calculating the global weighted Jensen-Shannon divergence global_js_divergence logical(kind=c_bool), intent(out), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: tmp_neighbor_mask_S1 Work array for the mask selecting study 1's neighbors that belong to family_idx logical(kind=c_bool), intent(out), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: tmp_neighbor_mask_S2 Work array for the mask selecting study 2's neighbors that belong to family_idx real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_pmf_S1 Work array for study 1's normalized histogram counts real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_pmf_S2 Work array for study 2's normalized histogram counts integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_counts Work array for the histogram counts","tags":"","url":"proc/fjct_compute_jsd_impl.html"},{"title":"fjct_compute_masked_jsd_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine fjct_compute_masked_jsd_impl(neighborhood_residuals_S1, neighborhood_residuals_S2, n_reps_S1, n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points, neighbor_mask_S1, neighbor_mask_S2, n_bins, shared_residual_range, js_divergences, included_n_reps_S1, included_n_reps_S2, total_included_n_reps, global_js_divergence, weights, pmf_S1, pmf_S2, tmp_counts) Reuses the same conditioning-on-mean-expression pipeline as the global gJCT, but restricts the\nresidual samples to the neighbors selected by neighbor_mask_S1 / neighbor_mask_S2 . Typically\nthose are all neighbors belonging to one gene family, which is what fjct_compute_jsd builds\nthe masks for from a family index. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S1, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S1 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 1, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(n_reps_S2, n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighborhood_residuals_S2 Computed neighborhood residuals for study 2, NaN is explicitly allowed for missing values\nNaN is permitted for this value. integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S1 Number of replicates in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_reps_S2 Number of replicates in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_neighbors Number of neighbors in the studies integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_points Number of reference points in the studies logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S1 Mask selecting the neighbors of study 1 to include logical(kind=c_bool), intent(in), dimension(n_neighbors, n_points) :: neighbor_mask_S2 Mask selecting the neighbors of study 2 to include integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: n_bins Number of equally sized histogram bins used for the studies real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: shared_residual_range Computed residual range for both studies\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: js_divergences Jensen-Shannon divergence per reference point, computed for studies S1 and S2 integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: included_n_reps_S1 Count of non-NaN residuals (included ones) in study 1 integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: included_n_reps_S2 Count of non-NaN residuals (included ones) in study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: total_included_n_reps Total number of included replicates from both studies ( ) real(kind=real64), intent(out) :: global_js_divergence Weighted global Jensen-Shannon divergence real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points) :: weights Weights used for calculating the global weighted Jensen-Shannon divergence global_js_divergence real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: pmf_S1 Normalized histogram counts for study 1 real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: pmf_S2 Normalized histogram counts for study 2 integer(kind=int32), intent(out), dimension(n_points, n_bins) :: tmp_counts Work array for the histogram counts","tags":"","url":"proc/fjct_compute_masked_jsd_impl.html"},{"title":"fjct_compute_contribution_scores_impl – TensorOmics","text":"public pure subroutine fjct_compute_contribution_scores_impl(global_js_divergences, total_included_n_reps_per_f, k_families, support_weights, contribution_scores) Combines how divergent the family is between the studies ( global_js_divergences ), and how much residual support the family has overall ( total_included_n_reps_per_f ), using the outputs of fjct_compute_jsd , collected for the analyzed sub-neighborhoods. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in), dimension(k_families) :: global_js_divergences Per-sub-neighborhood weighted global JSD\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in), dimension(k_families) :: total_included_n_reps_per_f Per-sub-neighborhood total_included_n_reps The minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: k_families Number of sub-neighborhoods analyzed real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(k_families) :: support_weights Per-sub-neighborhood calculated support weight (ratio between its total_included_n_reps and sum(total_included_n_reps_per_f) , zero if there were no replicates included at all) real(kind=real64), intent(out), dimension(k_families) :: contribution_scores Per-sub-neighborhood calculated contribution ( )","tags":"","url":"proc/fjct_compute_contribution_scores_impl.html"},{"title":"normalize_variable_timeseries_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine normalize_variable_timeseries_c(v, v_norm, n_points, status, ierr) bind(C, name=\"normalize_variable_timeseries_c\") Uses tox_trajectory_normalization Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_points), target :: v Original time series real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: v_norm Normalized time series integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Vector length (number of time points) integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: status Status code for specific warnings integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_variable_timeseries_c.html"},{"title":"normalize_single_trajectory_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine normalize_single_trajectory_c(trajectory, trajectory_norm, n_factors, n_timepoints, status, ierr) bind(C, name=\"normalize_single_trajectory_c\") Uses tox_trajectory_normalization Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_timepoints, n_factors), target :: trajectory Original trajectory for one sample real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_timepoints, n_factors), target :: trajectory_norm Normalized trajectory for one sample integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_factors Number of factors/variables integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_timepoints Number of time points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_factors), target :: status Status code for specific warnings, one per factor integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_single_trajectory_c.html"},{"title":"normalize_all_trajectories_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine normalize_all_trajectories_c(trajectories, trajectories_norm, n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints, status, ierr) bind(C, name=\"normalize_all_trajectories_c\") Uses tox_trajectory_normalization independently across time for each sample. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints), target :: trajectories Original trajectories real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints), target :: trajectories_norm Normalized trajectories integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_factors Number of factors integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_samples Number of samples/entities integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_timepoints Number of time points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_factors, n_samples), target :: status Status code for specific warnings, one per factor per sample integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_all_trajectories_c.html"},{"title":"normalize_all_trajectories_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine normalize_all_trajectories_expert_c(trajectories, trajectories_norm, n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints, tmp_series, tmp_series_norm, status, ierr) bind(C, name=\"normalize_all_trajectories_expert_c\") Uses tox_trajectory_normalization independently across time for each sample. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints), target :: trajectories Original trajectories real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_factors, n_samples, n_timepoints), target :: trajectories_norm Normalized trajectories integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_factors Number of factors integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_samples Number of samples/entities integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_timepoints Number of time points real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_timepoints), target :: tmp_series Work array: one factor's time series, gathered contiguously real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_timepoints), target :: tmp_series_norm Work array: the normalized time series integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_factors, n_samples), target :: status Status code for specific warnings, one per factor per sample integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/normalize_all_trajectories_expert_c.html"},{"title":"build_kd_index_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine build_kd_index_c(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, ierr) bind(C, name=\"build_kd_index_c\") Uses f42_kd_tree Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points), target :: points Data points integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions), target :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/build_kd_index_c.html"},{"title":"build_kd_index_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine build_kd_index_expert_c(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, tmp_workspace, tmp_value_buffer, tmp_permutation, tmp_recursion_stack, ierr) bind(C, name=\"build_kd_index_expert_c\") Uses f42_kd_tree Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points), target :: points Data points integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions), target :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_workspace Workspace array real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_value_buffer Workspace for sorting integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_permutation Workspace for sorting integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(3, n_points), target :: tmp_recursion_stack Stack for l, r, depth integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/build_kd_index_expert_c.html"},{"title":"build_spherical_kd_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine build_spherical_kd_c(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, ierr) bind(C, name=\"build_spherical_kd_c\") Uses f42_kd_tree This is a thin, semantically-named wrapper: partitioning is identical to build_kd_index_impl (plain per-axis median splits);\ncallers are responsible for ensuring points are actually unit-normalized beforehand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points), target :: points Data points integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions), target :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/build_spherical_kd_c.html"},{"title":"build_spherical_kd_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine build_spherical_kd_expert_c(points, n_dimensions, n_points, kd_indices, dimension_order, tmp_workspace, tmp_value_buffer, tmp_permutation, tmp_recursion_stack, ierr) bind(C, name=\"build_spherical_kd_expert_c\") Uses f42_kd_tree This is a thin, semantically-named wrapper: partitioning is identical to build_kd_index_impl (plain per-axis median splits);\ncallers are responsible for ensuring points are actually unit-normalized beforehand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions, n_points), target :: points Data points integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dimensions Number of dimensions integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_points Number of points integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: kd_indices Output index array (k-d tree order) integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_dimensions), target :: dimension_order Dimension order (by variance)\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_dimensions . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_workspace Workspace array real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_value_buffer Workspace for sorting integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_points), target :: tmp_permutation Workspace for sorting integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(3, n_points), target :: tmp_recursion_stack Stack for l, r, depth integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/build_spherical_kd_expert_c.html"},{"title":"detect_neofunctionalization_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine detect_neofunctionalization_c(ancestors, n_families, genes, n_axes, gene_to_fam, n_genes, thresholds, neofunc, ierr) bind(C, name=\"detect_neofunctionalization_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_families), target :: ancestors RAP projected unit length expression vector of ancestral ortholog\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_families number of vectors in ancestors real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes, n_genes), target :: genes RAP projected unit length expression vectors of genes\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_axes size of ancestors vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_to_fam Index mapping -> each index i holds the family index for the corresponding gene in genes , using 0_int32 for unassigned genes\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families .\nThe value 0_int32 is additionally accepted. integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of vectors in genes real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_axes), target :: thresholds threshold per axis that defines significant change in expression, may be a percentile of all genes' changes per axis\nThe minimum valid value is -1.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is 1.0_real64 . logical(kind=c_bool), intent(out), dimension(n_genes, n_axes), target :: neofunc .true. if neofunctionalization has been detected for the respective axes, always .false. for unassigned genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code; zero on success, non-zero on failure.","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_neofunctionalization_c.html"},{"title":"detect_dosage_effect_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine detect_dosage_effect_c(ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, n_results, max_subset_size, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, max_angle, gain_gamma, ierr) bind(C, name=\"detect_dosage_effect_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes), target :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask with the genes' indices kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0. Build it with the matching filter_paralogs_by_pattern_* routine integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes. Use subroutine mask_chunk_count for calculation\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_results number of resulting subsets. They are stored as the first n_results elements of work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_subset_size maximum subset size of checked gene subsets. Too large a value is capped to the\nmaximum valid size. The bindings cap it automatically while sizing the work\narray; a Fortran caller caps it by calling calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size first.\nZero is in range and means there is no subset to check -- the sizing routine reports\nit whenever the filtered families hold a single gene each. It reports a work array\nof zero slots along with it, which this routine does not accept, so a caller that\ngets zero back has nothing to detect and should not call here at all.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets), target :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. Note Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets .\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the work_array_size output produced by calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: max_angle maximum angle in radians 0<=angle<=Pi that a subset candidate must not exceed, otherwise pruned\nThe default value is 4.0_real64*atan(1.0_real64) .\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: gain_gamma positive magnitude gain for dosage effect\nThe default value is 0.1_real64 .\nThe minimum valid value is above(0.0_real64) . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_dosage_effect_c.html"},{"title":"detect_dosage_effect_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine detect_dosage_effect_expert_c(ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, n_results, max_subset_size, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, tmp_active_mask, tmp_paralog_vector, max_angle, gain_gamma, ierr) bind(C, name=\"detect_dosage_effect_expert_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes), target :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask with the genes' indices kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0. Build it with the matching filter_paralogs_by_pattern_* routine integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes. Use subroutine mask_chunk_count for calculation\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_results number of resulting subsets. They are stored as the first n_results elements of work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_subset_size maximum subset size of checked gene subsets. Too large a value is capped to the\nmaximum valid size. The bindings cap it automatically while sizing the work\narray; a Fortran caller caps it by calling calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size first.\nZero is in range and means there is no subset to check -- the sizing routine reports\nit whenever the filtered families hold a single gene each. It reports a work array\nof zero slots along with it, which this routine does not accept, so a caller that\ngets zero back has nothing to detect and should not call here at all.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets), target :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. Note Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets .\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the work_array_size output produced by calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: tmp_active_mask working array to hold the extended subsets real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_dims), target :: tmp_paralog_vector vector used for pruning subsets real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: max_angle maximum angle in radians 0<=angle<=Pi that a subset candidate must not exceed, otherwise pruned\nThe default value is 4.0_real64*atan(1.0_real64) .\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: gain_gamma positive magnitude gain for dosage effect\nThe default value is 0.1_real64 .\nThe minimum valid value is above(0.0_real64) . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_dosage_effect_expert_c.html"},{"title":"detect_subfunctionalization_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine detect_subfunctionalization_c(ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, n_results, max_subset_size, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, rdi_threshold, paralog_norms, sorted_paralog_norms_perm, ierr) bind(C, name=\"detect_subfunctionalization_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes), target :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask with the genes' indices kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0. Build it with the matching filter_paralogs_by_pattern_* routine integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes. Use subroutine mask_chunk_count for calculation\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_results number of resulting subsets. They are stored as the first n_results elements of work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_subset_size maximum subset size of checked gene subsets. Too large a value is capped to the\nmaximum valid size. The bindings cap it automatically while sizing the work\narray; a Fortran caller caps it by calling calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size first.\nZero is in range and means there is no subset to check -- the sizing routine reports\nit whenever the filtered families hold a single gene each. It reports a work array\nof zero slots along with it, which this routine does not accept, so a caller that\ngets zero back has nothing to detect and should not call here at all.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets), target :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. Note Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets .\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the work_array_size output produced by calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: rdi_threshold max allowed residual distance from ancestor The minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: paralog_norms euclidean norms of the genes, used for subset pruning ( norm from f42_vector_impl computes them)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: sorted_paralog_norms_perm ascending permutation of the norms, for subset pruning: the smallest norm among the genes that could extend a subset must not fall below the subset's angle to the ancestor\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_subfunctionalization_c.html"},{"title":"detect_subfunctionalization_expert_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine detect_subfunctionalization_expert_c(ancestor, genes, n_genes, n_dims, filtered_paralogs_mask, n_mask_chunks, n_results, max_subset_size, work_arr_paralog_subsets, n_paralog_subsets, tmp_active_mask, tmp_paralog_vector, rdi_threshold, paralog_norms, sorted_paralog_norms_perm, tmp_work_array, ierr) bind(C, name=\"detect_subfunctionalization_expert_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims), target :: ancestor expression vector of ancestral ortholog real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_dims, n_genes), target :: genes expression vectors of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_dims size of ancestor vector and vectors in genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: filtered_paralogs_mask bit mask with the genes' indices kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0. Build it with the matching filter_paralogs_by_pattern_* routine integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes. Use subroutine mask_chunk_count for calculation\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: n_results number of resulting subsets. They are stored as the first n_results elements of work_arr_paralog_subsets integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: max_subset_size maximum subset size of checked gene subsets. Too large a value is capped to the\nmaximum valid size. The bindings cap it automatically while sizing the work\narray; a Fortran caller caps it by calling calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size first.\nZero is in range and means there is no subset to check -- the sizing routine reports\nit whenever the filtered families hold a single gene each. It reports a work array\nof zero slots along with it, which this routine does not accept, so a caller that\ngets zero back has nothing to detect and should not call here at all.\nThe minimum valid value is 0_int32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_paralog_subsets), target :: work_arr_paralog_subsets working array to hold bitmask encoded subsets for detection. Note Each bitmask is built of 32 bit chunks. (n_genes + 31) / 32 is equivalent to ceil(n_genes / 32.0_real64) and represents the number of chunks integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_paralog_subsets number of gene subsets that can be stored in work_arr_paralog_subsets .\nIt is VERY IMPORTANT to compute this argument from the work_array_size output produced by calc_work_arr_paralog_subsets_size . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks), target :: tmp_active_mask working array to hold the extended subsets real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_dims), target :: tmp_paralog_vector vector used for pruning subsets real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: rdi_threshold max allowed residual distance from ancestor The minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: paralog_norms euclidean norms of the genes, used for subset pruning ( norm from f42_vector_impl computes them)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: sorted_paralog_norms_perm ascending permutation of the norms, for subset pruning: the smallest norm among the genes that could extend a subset must not fall below the subset's angle to the ancestor\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . real(kind=c_double), intent(out), dimension(n_genes), target :: tmp_work_array work array for checking the minimum value after a given index efficiently integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/detect_subfunctionalization_expert_c.html"},{"title":"filter_paralogs_by_pattern_dosage_effect_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine filter_paralogs_by_pattern_dosage_effect_c(gene_angles, threshold, n_genes, n_families, gene_to_fam, masks, n_mask_chunks, ierr) bind(C, name=\"filter_paralogs_by_pattern_dosage_effect_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis This subroutine prefilters the genes for a specific pattern to reduce detection overhead, as less subsets need to be tried. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_angles vector, holding the angles between ancestor and genes (0<=angle<=Pi)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: threshold filter threshold integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_families number of families\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_to_fam a mapping of gene index to family index, so gene i is related to gene_angles(i) and part of family j=gene_to_fam(i) .\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_families), target :: masks bit mask that will have the indices of genes kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/filter_paralogs_by_pattern_dosage_effect_c.html"},{"title":"filter_paralogs_by_pattern_subfunctionalization_c – TensorOmics","text":"public  subroutine filter_paralogs_by_pattern_subfunctionalization_c(gene_angles, threshold, n_genes, n_families, gene_to_fam, masks, n_mask_chunks, ierr) bind(C, name=\"filter_paralogs_by_pattern_subfunctionalization_c\") Uses tox_paralog_analysis This subroutine prefilters the genes for a specific pattern to reduce detection overhead, as less subsets need to be tried. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=c_double), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_angles vector, holding the angles between ancestor and genes (0<=angle<=Pi)\nThe minimum valid value is 0.0_real64 .\nThe maximum valid value is PI . real(kind=c_double), intent(in), target :: threshold filter threshold integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_genes number of genes integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_families number of families\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_genes . integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), dimension(n_genes), target :: gene_to_fam a mapping of gene index to family index, so gene i is related to gene_angles(i) and part of family j=gene_to_fam(i) .\nThe minimum valid value is 1_int32 .\nThe maximum valid value is n_families . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), dimension(n_mask_chunks, n_families), target :: masks bit mask that will have the indices of genes kept by this pattern set to 1, else 0 integer(kind=c_int), intent(in), target :: n_mask_chunks number of 32 bit chunks a mask needs to encode n_genes genes\nThe minimum valid value is (n_genes + 31) / 32 . integer(kind=c_int), intent(out), target :: ierr Error code","tags":"","url":"proc/filter_paralogs_by_pattern_subfunctionalization_c.html"},{"title":"deserialize_real_helper_c